revision.text:
'''Yayoi Burial Mounds''' (弥生墳丘墓) refer to burial mounds with raised earthen structures from the {{ill|Yayoi period|ja|弥生時代}} of Japan{{efn|Defined as per {{harv|Wada|2003}} mentioned below.}}. These represent one of the {{ill|Yayoi period funerary practices|ja|弥生時代の墓制}}.

The evolution and development of elements present in Yayoi burial mounds are believed to have led to the emergence of the {{ill|kofun|ja|古墳}} of the {{ill|Kofun period|ja|古墳時代}}<ref>{{harv|Kondō|1983}}, {{harv|Kondō|1986}}, among others</ref>.

== Overview ==

In the early Yayoi period, burial mounds with earthworks started to be constructed, forming circular or square shapes.

By the late Yayoi period, burial mounds with earthen structures excavated after mound construction appeared. During this time, the mounds became larger, and features such as projecting sections started to emerge. These developments are believed to have been precursors to the kofun of the Kofun period.

=== Definition ===

The concept of Yayoi burial mounds was first proposed in {{ill|1977|ja|1977年}} by {{ill|Yoshirō Kondō|ja|近藤義郎}}{{sfn|Kondō|1977}}. Kondō distinguished burial mounds (or Yayoi burial mounds) as a group defined primarily by "earthen mounds delineating burial areas" from {{ill|trench graves|ja|周溝墓}} defined by surrounding ditches and {{ill|platform tombs|ja|台状墓}} characterized by a "carved-out platform around the burial area"{{sfn|Kondō|1977|p=1}}{{efn|Kondō noted that strict distinctions between these three types were challenging{{sfn|Kondō|1977|pp=1-2}} and considered this term provisional at the time.}}.

In contrast, {{ill|Hiroshi Tsude|ja|都出比呂志}} argued that "the degree of earthen mound coverage alone does not qualitatively distinguish square-ditched graves, square platform graves, and burial mounds"{{sfn|Tsude|1979|p=19}}, based on examples like the {{ill|Uryūdō site|ja|瓜生堂遺跡}} in Osaka Prefecture. Tsude proposed the term "burial mounds" to collectively refer to "tombs with raised earth structures"{{sfn|Tsude|1986|p=222}}, including kofun, and attempted to classify them further{{sfn|Tsude|1979}},{{sfn|Tsude|1986}}{{efn|Tsude categorized the "low burial mounds" of the Kofun period within a transhistorical context{{harv|Tsude|1986|p=224}}. He emphasized that these "low burial mounds," when belonging to the Kofun period, acquire the "special designation of 'kofun'"{{harv|Tsude|1986|p=224}}. However, the term "Yayoi burial mound" is not used in this paper.}}.

By {{ill|2003|ja|2003年}}, Tsude's definition had gained some academic support{{sfn|Wada|2003|p=3}}. However, there was still considerable variation in the terminology used by individual researchers{{sfn|Wada|2003|p=4}}. {{ill|Seigo Wada|ja|和田晴吾}} attempted to clarify the terms, using "burial mounds" as a general term for "tombs with mounds" and specifically designating those from the Yayoi period as "'''Yayoi burial mounds'''." Wada further classified these based on mound shape, construction methods, location, combinations with other grave types, and burial pit construction techniques{{sfn|Wada|2003}}.

=== Mound Shapes ===

The basic shapes include square-ditched mounds, circular-ditched mounds, square platform mounds, and circular platform mounds{{sfn|Wada|2003|pp=7-8}}. Some regions exhibit differentiation in the use of trench and platform graves{{sfn|Wada|2003|p=9}}. Variants include {{ill|square stone-covered mounds|ja|方形貼石墓}} and {{ill|four-cornered projecting burial mounds|ja|四隅突出型墳丘墓}}{{sfn|Wada|2003|pp=8-10}}.

