'''Yayoi Kofuns''' (弥生墳丘墓) refer to Kofuns with raised earthen structures from the {{ill|Yayoi period|ja|弥生時代|qq=draft|qq=draft}} of Japan{{efn|Defined as per {{harv|Wada|2003}} mentioned below.}}. These represent one of the {{ill|Yayoi period funerary practices|ja|弥生時代の墓制|1=Funerary practices of the Yayoi period|qq=draft|qq=draft}}.

The evolution and development of elements present in Yayoi Kofuns are believed to have led to the emergence of the {{ill|kofun|ja|古墳|1=Kofun|qq=draft|qq=draft}} of the {{ill|Kofun period|ja|古墳時代|qq=draft|qq=draft}}<ref>{{harv|Kondō|1983}}, {{harv|Kondō|1986}}, among others</ref>.

== Overview ==
During the early Yayoi period, Kofuns with soil heaped over burial chambers began to be constructed, forming circular and square mounds.

In the late Yayoi period, a new form emerged where the mound (heaped soil) was constructed first, followed by the excavation of the burial pit. This period also saw the enlargement of Kofuns and the formation of protruding sections, which are thought to have been carried over into the kofun (ancient tomb) of the Kofun period.

=== Definition ===
The concept of Yayoi Kofuns was first proposed by {{ill|Kondō Yoshio|ja|近藤義郎|qq=draft|qq=draft}} in 1977{{sfn|Kondō|1977}}. Kondō defined Yayoi Kofuns as "a group of tombs that appear to delineate and form the burial area primarily through heaped soil," distinguishing them from {{ill|moated graves|ja|周溝墓|qq=draft|qq=draft}} that define burial areas "primarily through trenches" and {{ill|terraced tombs|ja|台状墓|qq=draft|qq=draft}} that define burial areas "primarily by surrounding earth-cut areas"{{sfn|Kondō|1977|p=1}}{{efn|Kondō noted that it was difficult to strictly separate these three types and regarded the terms as provisional at the time{{sfn|Kondō|1977|pp=1-2}}.}}.

