In the late Yayoi period's latter half to its terminal phase, some Kofuns developed prominent protrusions, leading to the establishment of exclusive mound shapes for chieftain tombs. Meanwhile, other Kofuns became smaller, and lower-class graves evolved into densely packed pit graves. This period saw rapid social stratification within communities, leading to the disintegration of moated settlements. Prominent Kofuns began to serve as nuclei for regional and eventually interregional alliances. The adoption of shared Kofun styles at these levels likely distinguished these regions from others{{sfn|Wada|2004}}.

=== From Yayoi Kofuns to Keyhole-Shaped Tombs ===
The development of protrusions resembling the early form of front sections of keyhole-shaped mounds began in the latter half of the middle Yayoi period in the Chugoku Mountains, in the latter half of the late Yayoi period in San'in, slightly later in Hokuriku, and from the latter half of the late Yayoi period in Kibi.

Thus, by the latter half of the late Yayoi period, Yayoi Kofuns had evolved into distinct regional forms, reflecting the unique ritualistic and political landscapes of each area.

In the Kofun period, keyhole-shaped mounds grew larger, with protrusions forming refined front sections. Disparities in mound shapes and sizes became more pronounced, culminating in the formation of the '''{{ill|Kofun system|en|Kofun system|WD=Q11397224|lt=Kofun system|1=Keyhole tomb order}}''', encompassing keyhole-shaped, rectangular, circular, and square tombs{{sfn|Watanabe|2007}}.
== Regional Yayoi Kofuns ==
=== Northern Kyushu ===
The Kofun at the {{ill|Yoshinogari site|en|Yoshinogari site|WD=Q1049645|lt=Yoshinogari Site|1=Yoshinogari site}} in {{ill|Saga Prefecture|en|Saga Prefecture|WD=Q160420|lt=Saga Prefecture|1=Saga Prefecture}} is estimated to have been nearly rectangular, measuring approximately 46 meters north-south and 27 meters east-west, with a height of over 4.5 meters. The burial pit contained more than 14 jar burials, which date back to the middle Yayoi period. The reason for the emergence of large-scale Kofuns during this period remains unclear.

In the latter half of the middle Yayoi period, royal tombs began to appear. The Kofun at the {{ill|Mikumo Nanokoji Site|ja|三雲南小路遺跡|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Mikumo Nanokoji Site|qq=}} in {{ill|Itoshima, Fukuoka|en|Itoshima, Fukuoka|WD=Q1011145|lt=Itoshima City|1=Itoshima}}, {{ill|Fukuoka Prefecture|en|Fukuoka Prefecture|WD=Q123258|lt=Fukuoka Prefecture|1=Fukuoka Prefecture}}, is a moated square tomb over 30 meters on each side, believed to be a royal tomb. Fifty-seven or more Chinese bronze mirrors were excavated from two jar burials.

In the late Yayoi period, at the {{ill|Hirabaru Ruins|ja|平原遺跡|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hirabaru Site|comment=enwiki is a redirect}} in the same Itoshima City, the first tomb contained 40 bronze mirrors, including a large {{ill|Large flower mirror|en|Large flower mirror|WD=Q11433747|lt=inner-patterned flower mirror|1=Large flower mirror}} as grave goods.

=== Seto Inland Sea Region ===
The Yayoi Kofun at {{ill|Yokuyama Site No. 5|ja|養久山5号弥生墳丘墓|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yokuyama Site No. 5|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} in {{ill|Hyōgo Prefecture|en|Hyōgo Prefecture|WD=Q130290|lt=Hyōgo Prefecture|1=Hyōgo Prefecture}} has an estimated mound length of about 20 meters with protrusions at both ends, standing approximately 1.5 to 2 meters high.

The {{ill|Tatetsuki Site|en|Tatetsuki Site|WD=Q11541408|lt=Tatetsuki Site|1=Tatetsuki Site}} in {{ill|Kurashiki|en|Kurashiki|WD=Q467487|lt=Kurashiki City|1=Kurashiki}}, {{ill|Okayama Prefecture|en|Okayama Prefecture|WD=Q132936|lt=Okayama Prefecture|1=Okayama Prefecture}}, is large, measuring 80 meters in mound length (including protrusions) and about 4.5 meters in height. The protrusions are encircled by two rows of standing stones.

=== San'in Region ===
From the northern part of {{ill|Hiroshima Prefecture|en|Hiroshima Prefecture|WD=Q617375|lt=Hiroshima Prefecture|1=Hiroshima Prefecture}} through the {{ill|Chūgoku Mountains|en|Chūgoku Mountains|WD=Q857567|lt=Chugoku Mountains|1=Chūgoku Mountains}} to northern {{ill|Kansai region|en|Kansai region|WD=Q164256|lt=Kinki Region|1=Kansai region}}, {{ill|stone-lined square tombs|ja|方形貼石墓|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/stone-lined square tombs|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} began to be constructed during the middle Yayoi period{{sfn|Higo|2010}}.

In the latter half of the middle Yayoi period, {{ill|X-shaped Burial Mound|lt=X-shaped Burial Mounds|ja|四隅突出型弥生墳丘墓|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/X-shaped Burial Mound|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 四隅突出型墳丘墓}} developed in the {{ill|Chūgoku Mountains|en|Chūgoku Mountains|WD=Q857567|lt=Chugoku Mountains|1=Chūgoku Mountains}} and {{ill|Izumo Plain|ja|出雲平野|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Izumo Plain|1=Izumo Plain|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11395890}}, becoming prominent burial sites throughout the San'in region, including the {{ill|Tottori Plain|ja|鳥取平野|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tottori Plain|1=Tottori Plain|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11674508}} and {{ill|Oki Islands|ja|隠岐|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Oki Islands|1=Oki Province|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11659029}}. At the {{ill|Nishidani Tumulus cluster|en|Nishidani Tumulus cluster|WD=Q11629514|lt=Nishidani Kofun Group|1=Nishidani Kofun cluster}} in {{ill|Izumo, Shimane|en|Izumo, Shimane|WD=Q825166|lt=Izumo City|1=Izumo, Shimane}}, {{ill|Shimane Prefecture|en|Shimane Prefecture|WD=Q132751|lt=Shimane Prefecture|1=Shimane Prefecture}}, six protruded-corner mounds were constructed. Among them, Tomb No. 3 measured approximately 40 meters east-west, 30 meters north-south, and about 4.5 meters in height. The {{ill|Arashima Kofun Cluster|ja|荒島墳墓群|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Arashima Kofun Cluster|qq=}} in {{ill|Yasugi, Shimane|en|Yasugi, Shimane|WD=Q851114|lt=Yasugi City|1=Yasugi, Shimane}} is the largest concentration of protruded-corner mounds in Japan and saw continuous construction of various chieftain tombs through the Kofun period{{efn|This cluster includes the {{ill|Nakasenji Kofun Group|ja|仲仙寺墳墓群|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nakasenji Kofun Group|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}, Anyoji Kofun Group, Shiozuyama Kofun Group, and Shimoyama Kofun Group.}}.

=== Northern Kinki ===
In the northern {{ill|Kansai region|en|Kansai region|WD=Q164256|lt=Kinki Region|1=Kansai region}}, the {{ill|Tango Province|en|Tango Province|WD=Q1054331|lt=Tango Province|1=Tango Province}} area saw the emergence of large Kofuns with luxurious grave goods during the terminal Yayoi period. One such example is the {{ill|Akasakaimai Tumuli|en|Akasakaimai Tumuli|WD=Q11635401|lt=Akasakaimai Tumuli|1=Akasakaimai Tumuli}} in {{ill|Kyoto Prefecture|en|Kyoto Prefecture|WD=Q120730|lt=Kyoto Prefecture|1=Kyoto Prefecture}}, measuring 37 by 35 meters and about 4.2 meters in height. A large burial pit was discovered at its summit, but no coffin has been unearthed. Based on excavated pottery, the mound dates to the early 3rd century and is believed to be the tomb of a political leader in the region. Despite its location along the {{ill|Sea of Japan|en|Sea of Japan|WD=Q27092|lt=Sea of Japan|1=Sea of Japan}}, the Tango area lacks evidence of protruded-corner mounds found in other regions like San'in and Hokuriku.

=== Central Kinki ===
The {{ill|Hokenoyama Kofun|en|Hokenoyama Kofun|WD=Q11337664|lt=Hokenoyama Kofun|1=Hokenoyama Kofun}} in {{ill|Nara Prefecture|en|Nara Prefecture|WD=Q131287|lt=Nara Prefecture|1=Nara Prefecture}} is a Kofun with a keyhole-shaped plan. It is debated whether it represents a Yayoi Kofun or belongs to the Kofun period{{efn|Kaoru Terasawa includes this mound among six {{ill|Makimuku-type keyhole-shaped kofun|ja|纒向型前方後円墳|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Makimuku-type keyhole-shaped kofun|qq=}} constructed prior to the Hashihaka Kofun{{sfn|Terasawa|1988}}.}}.

=== Hokuriku ===
In the late Yayoi period, protruded-corner Kofuns were also constructed in the {{ill|Hokuriku region|en|Hokuriku region|WD=Q381025|lt=Hokuriku Region|1=Hokuriku region}}{{sfn|Furukawa|1993}}.

